  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'What is the causative agent of tuberculosis (TB)? What laboratory tests would you do to confirm the diagnosis? What do you understand by the term acid-fast bacilli? (3 marks)', 'Causative agent: Mycobacterium tuberculosis, a slow-growing, rod-shaped bacterium.
Laboratory tests: (1) sputum smear microscopy - two sputum samples stained by the Ziehl-Neelsen method and examined for acid-fast bacilli; (2) GeneXpert (cartridge-based nucleic acid amplification), which detects TB DNA within hours and also shows rifampicin resistance; (3) sputum culture (Löwenstein-Jensen medium or liquid culture) with drug-sensitivity testing. Chest X-ray supports the diagnosis; the Mantoux test or IGRA shows infection, not active disease.
Acid-fast bacilli (AFB): bacteria such as mycobacteria whose cell walls contain waxy mycolic acids. Once stained with hot carbol fuchsin they resist decolourisation with acid-alcohol, so in the Ziehl-Neelsen stain they appear as red rods against a blue background.', null, '[{"point": "Causative agent: Mycobacterium tuberculosis", "marks": 0.5}, {"point": "Tests: sputum smear microscopy with Ziehl-Neelsen stain (2 samples), GeneXpert (CBNAAT) which also detects rifampicin resistance, sputum culture; supported by chest X-ray (Mantoux/IGRA for infection)", "marks": 1.5}, {"point": "Acid-fast bacilli: bacteria (mycobacteria) with waxy mycolic acid cell walls that retain carbol fuchsin stain even after washing with acid-alcohol, appearing red/pink on a blue background", "marks": 1}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge') and t.slug = 'medical-science-general-subject-knowledge'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-2'), 'standard', 3,
