  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Define the terms digestion and assimilation. Name the enzyme that digests fats. Why are proteases generally released in an inactive form? (3 marks)', 'Digestion is the process by which large, complex, insoluble food molecules are broken down - mechanically and by enzymes - into small, soluble molecules (glucose, amino acids, fatty acids and glycerol) that can be absorbed.
Assimilation is the process by which the absorbed food molecules are taken up and used by body cells - to build new protoplasm, for growth and repair, or to release energy - so they become part of the body.
The enzyme that digests fats is lipase (mainly pancreatic lipase; bile salts emulsify fat first).
Proteases are secreted as inactive precursors (zymogens), such as pepsinogen and trypsinogen, because in active form they would digest the proteins of the very cells and ducts that produce them (autodigestion). They are activated only in the lumen of the stomach (pepsinogen by HCl) or intestine (trypsinogen by enterokinase).', null, '[{"point": "Digestion: breakdown of large insoluble food molecules into small soluble ones by enzymes (and mechanical action) so they can be absorbed", "marks": 0.75}, {"point": "Assimilation: use of absorbed food molecules by body cells to build new tissue/release energy (becoming part of the body)", "marks": 0.75}, {"point": "Enzyme digesting fats: lipase (pancreatic lipase), helped by bile salts", "marks": 0.5}, {"point": "Proteases are released as inactive zymogens (pepsinogen, trypsinogen) so they do not digest the cells and glands that make them (autodigestion); they are activated only in the gut lumen", "marks": 1}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge') and t.slug = 'medical-science-general-subject-knowledge'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-2'), 'standard', 3,
