  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Define refractive error. What are short-sightedness and long-sightedness? How is each corrected? (3 marks)', 'Refractive error is a condition in which the eye cannot bring parallel light rays to a sharp focus on the retina, so vision is blurred. It is due to the shape of the eyeball or the focusing power of the cornea and lens.
Short-sightedness (myopia): near objects are seen clearly but distant objects are blurred, because the image of a distant object forms in front of the retina - the eyeball is too long or the lens too powerful. It is corrected with a concave (diverging) lens.
Long-sightedness (hypermetropia): distant objects are clear but near objects are blurred, because the image forms behind the retina - the eyeball is too short or the lens too weak. It is corrected with a convex (converging) lens.
(Contact lenses or refractive laser surgery can also correct these errors.)', null, '[{"point": "Refractive error: the eye fails to focus light rays exactly on the retina, so the image is blurred (due to eyeball length or lens/cornea power)", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Short-sightedness (myopia): distant objects blurred; image forms in front of the retina (eyeball too long/lens too strong); corrected with a concave (diverging) lens", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Long-sightedness (hypermetropia): near objects blurred; image forms behind the retina (eyeball too short/lens too weak); corrected with a convex (converging) lens", "marks": 1}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
