  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Differentiate between isometric and isotonic exercise. Outline one use of ultrasound and one use of infrared rays in physiotherapy. (3 marks)', 'Isometric exercise: the muscle contracts and produces tension but its length does not change and the joint does not move - e.g. pressing against a wall, static quadriceps contractions in a patient with a knee injury. Isotonic exercise: the muscle shortens (concentric) or lengthens (eccentric) and moves the joint through a range of motion against a constant load - e.g. lifting weights, squats, walking.
Ultrasound in physiotherapy: high-frequency sound waves produce deep heating and micro-massage of tissues, used to relieve pain, reduce swelling and speed healing of soft-tissue injuries such as sprains, tendinitis and bursitis.
Infrared rays in physiotherapy: an infrared lamp gives superficial heat, used to relieve pain and muscle spasm, improve blood circulation and relax tissues before exercise or massage (e.g. in back pain, arthritis).', null, '[{"point": "Isometric: muscle contracts without change in length and no joint movement (e.g. pushing against a wall, static quadriceps); isotonic: muscle changes length and moves the joint against constant load (e.g. lifting weights, squats)", "marks": 1.5}, {"point": "Ultrasound therapy: deep heating/micro-massage to reduce pain and promote healing of soft-tissue injuries (sprains, tendinitis)", "marks": 0.75}, {"point": "Infrared therapy: superficial heating to relieve pain and muscle spasm and improve circulation before exercise/massage", "marks": 0.75}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
